  "description": "The Hunstanton Wolf Trail is a historic coastal walk that explores the legacy of St Edmund, the first patron saint of England, who landed here in 855 A.D. and ruled the Kingdom of East Anglia.The route begins at the Esplanade Gardens, marked by an informative display near the town\u2019s cenotaph, and guides you through six distinct waymarkers that trace the saint's journey from his arrival to his martyrdom at the hands of the Vikings. Along the way, the trail delves into the legend of the wolf that guarded Edmund\u2019s severed head, a story deeply tied to his family name, the Wuffingas.As you follow the path toward the dramatic ruins of St Edmund\u2019s Chapel on the cliff top, you\u2019ll discover how the town's identity was shaped by this history. In the 1840s, Henry Le Strange even founded his new sea-bathing village as \"St Edmunds,\" a name the town retained for nearly fifty years. The trail winds through beautifully landscaped gardens and culminates at the site of the striking Wolf statue, offering a blend of ancient folklore, Victorian heritage, and sweeping views of the iconic striped cliffs.",
